WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.419 --> 00:00:05.312
Jean-Claude Van Damme is the only dude

00:00:05.312 --> 00:00:07.163
that punches and flex.

00:00:07.163 --> 00:00:09.254
"Ah!"

00:00:09.254 --> 00:00:11.280
And you just looking at the muscle.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.070
You're not even looking at the move!

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.645
Hi, my name is Uriah
Hall. I am a UFC fighter,

00:00:15.645 --> 00:00:17.760
and my background is boxing, kickboxing,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.110
Kyokushinkai karate.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.070
I know wrestling and jujitsu.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.228
Today we're going to be looking at

00:00:22.228 --> 00:00:24.769
mixed-martial-arts
scenes in movies and TVs

00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:26.905
and judging how real they really are.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.490
He's throwing wild
punches in the beginning.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:36.850
That's not really a smart thing to do.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:38.010
It leaves yourself open.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.320
Of course, the big guy
will just capitalize on it.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.580
If I'm fighting someone
that's bigger than me,

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.605
movement is always going to be important,

00:00:44.605 --> 00:00:47.314
because for them to chase me,

00:00:47.314 --> 00:00:49.005
it's going to take them
out of their element.

00:00:49.005 --> 00:00:51.610
I recommend speed on a
person that's bigger than me.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:53.890
Any type of straight
technique down the pipe,

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.880
because strait technique is
always going to be faster

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:56.810
than round technique.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.560
Body shots will slow anybody down.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.540
I think of it like a tree.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.350
If I can chop him down at the root,

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.526
then I can finish him up top.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.500
Commentator: He's fallen
deep into a guillotine.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.717
With the guillotine that I know,

00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:10.160
you can either get him in here,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.110
sometimes you can get the
person's neck underneath here

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.320
and just kind of use your hips.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.211
It's more of a hip position.

00:01:16.211 --> 00:01:17.527
He had him against the octagon,

00:01:17.527 --> 00:01:19.240
so it's kind of hard to get your hips up

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.970
as someone's pressing into you.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.340
So it leaves you to
focus more on your hands.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.724
So that looks like a
pretty realistic position.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.660
He's way too small to pull off that move.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.200
If someone has your neck here,

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.340
that's the natural defense,
to kind of put your hand over.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.410
So naturally what you
have to do is go lower

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.350
to let the person roll underneath you,

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:44.042
but it's hard to pick the person up.

00:01:46.824 --> 00:01:48.440
So, the ground stuff looks pretty good.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:49.473
A little dramatic.

00:01:49.473 --> 00:01:51.850
If he's turning around
to get that arm bar,

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.160
he's stepping around like that.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.498
That's too much space.

00:01:54.498 --> 00:01:57.000
That person could just
rip their arm out easily.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.620
Because in grappling, you
gotta stay tight, very close.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.550
You know, there's no, none of this.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:03.710
The thing that I notice a
lot with mixed martial arts

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:04.750
or martial arts films,

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.470
they want that big dramatic effect.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:08.037
"Oh, my God, this is happening!"

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.180
So, that's a really good knee bar.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:17.720
I mean, it's very deep.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.400
He's got both legs around.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.290
He's got the person's
leg close to his head.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:22.190
That's perfect.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:23.750
It's dramatic how he's putting his hands

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:24.800
all the way behind him.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.810
That's just taking way too much energy.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.070
All you gotta do is just close your hand

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:30.150
and press your hip forward.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.740
I would definitely give
this a 8 out of 10.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:35.420
I think it's a great
introduction towards MMA

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.933
and a great story as well.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:44.270
I love this.

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:45.320
What he's doing right now,

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.610
he's throwing that big right hand,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.440
and he's throwing that right hand as well.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.656
He slips under.

00:02:51.656 --> 00:02:53.731
That's one of the most coolest thing

00:02:53.731 --> 00:02:56.843
you can do to grab
someone, to submit them.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:05.210
The person's choking you
with their arm on one side,

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.425
and you're choking yourself

00:03:07.425 --> 00:03:09.430
with your arm on one side right here.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.490
So if you get your arm here,

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.580
that's going to close my
arteries a little bit. Right?

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.010
And then no blood's going to go the brain,

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:17.185
and then I'm going to pass out.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:24.943
That's totally accurate,
believe it or not.

00:03:24.943 --> 00:03:27.690
So, he has a triangle leg lock.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.935
And if he has the arm, he
would just have to kind of

00:03:29.935 --> 00:03:32.370
put it in the middle
and press his hips up,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.920
and then you'll have
an arm bar from there.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.920
Or if the person turns into you,

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:38.788
you can transition to a triangle.

00:03:38.788 --> 00:03:41.950
But that is just great
control on the bottom.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:44.201
If I can get my legs around you like that,

00:03:44.201 --> 00:03:46.270
then I'm controlling you.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.758
So, a lot of people think

00:03:48.758 --> 00:03:51.270
if you're on top all the
time, you have an advantage.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:54.968
But if I can use my legs to pull you in

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:56.940
and control you, push and pull, right?

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:58.140
If I'm pushing your arm here,

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.370
I'm pulling you in under your armpit.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:02.060
I'm pushing your hips away.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.740
I'm pulling you with my arm.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.680
And if I can get my legs
around your waist here,

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:07.930
I can pull you in.

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:10.663
Now, normally if someone's on top,

00:04:10.663 --> 00:04:12.230
you can strike. But from here,

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.680
we notice that he has his leg locked.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.930
So now I have one arm free.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:17.820
I can't really do much.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.973
I can punch, but the person on the bottom

00:04:19.973 --> 00:04:22.615
is so far away, it's hard to hit them.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.122
This is textbook. This is perfect.

00:04:34.122 --> 00:04:37.450
If anyone double-legs you,
that's a natural defense.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:40.068
When someone grabs into your legs,

00:04:40.068 --> 00:04:41.680
their head is going to be exposed.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.590
Now, granted, when you do attack someone,

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.660
your head should be up
a little bit, right?

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.190
And that will make it a little harder.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:49.000
But even then, you're still at risk.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.545
So I think what Eren did right away,

00:04:50.545 --> 00:04:53.320
he just locked into guillotine
and locked the arm then,

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:56.040
and you notice he locked his leg.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.560
So now it's even harder
for this guy to get out.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.108
I mean, this is just perfect.

00:05:01.108 --> 00:05:03.266
This blows me away. This is 10 out of 10.

00:05:03.266 --> 00:05:04.413
I need to watch this.

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:11.540
I really love Jean-Claude Van Damme.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:14.240
He's definitely one of
the guys, if not the guy,

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.890
to inspire me to kick. Because,
you know, I'm a kicker.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.470
I've knocked out guys with spinning kicks.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:20.100
I've learned other cool kicks.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.200
When I was 12, this was life.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.050
Round kick's one of my favorite kicks.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:26.225
That was pretty accurate.

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:28.300
There are so many ways to do round kicks.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.470
If you're using the shin part of your leg,

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:33.060
I would recommend it
to you to chop the leg

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.270
or hit him in the body.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:38.036
You can hit him in the face
a little harder. Use shin.

00:05:40.739 --> 00:05:42.370
H--- no, this will never work.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.240
Nobody should ever stand there

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:47.160
and get kicked with the same kick.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:48.910
Put your hands up, champ!

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:50.867
In a real fight, I would definitely

00:05:50.867 --> 00:05:52.650
be throwing a lot of low kicks.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.000
As a fighter, I think more like a sniper.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.880
I'm the guy that waits, and
I look for that perfect shot,

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:00.560
and I take it. And I'm
very precise with it.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.670
I contribute that to my karate background,

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:06.270
because in karate, we
had to do tornado kicks

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.490
and kick apples off each other's heads.

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:10.920
So that's the type of
accuracy I had to have,

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.533
and it helped me throughout
the rest of my career.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:19.700
Jean-Claude Van Damme is the only dude

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.596
that punches and flex.

00:06:21.596 --> 00:06:23.662
"Ah!"

00:06:23.662 --> 00:06:25.690
And you just looking at the muscle.

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:27.198
You're not even looking at the move!

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:35.070
That's where I got that kick from.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.245
The spinning hook kick.

00:06:37.245 --> 00:06:38.970
So many ways to do this kick.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.000
What Jean-Claude Van
Damme just did right there

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.860
is an extension of his leg.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.900
The further that leg is out,

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.416
the more whip it's going to have,

00:06:47.416 --> 00:06:49.316
the stronger it's going to be.

00:06:49.316 --> 00:06:51.300
When I did this kick to
knock out my opponent

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.218
on "The Ultimate Fighter,"
I chambered my leg.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:57.850
So I spun,

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:01.030
but instead of extending
it, I kept it intact.

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:03.455
Now, that was because of the distance,

00:07:03.455 --> 00:07:05.905
because if he's close and I extend my leg,

00:07:05.905 --> 00:07:07.770
it's going to create that off balance.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:09.735
But in that split of a second,

00:07:09.735 --> 00:07:12.010
you have to recognize that distance.

00:07:13.465 --> 00:07:14.565
Oh!

00:07:15.443 --> 00:07:17.070
Pew!

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:21.068
I've been attempting that kick for years.

00:07:21.068 --> 00:07:22.075
Still haven't gotten it.

00:07:22.075 --> 00:07:24.020
You gotta be able to have
a good split to do that,

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:26.710
because what happened is
when you extend that leg,

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:28.680
you're using the other leg as momentum

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.570
to generate that power.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:32.670
Not as realistic as it seemed,

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.262
because it's so much
energy to generate that.

00:07:35.262 --> 00:07:38.153
Takes time to throw it, and in that time,

00:07:38.153 --> 00:07:41.530
that person could have a
defense mechanism to go,

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:42.455
"I'm outta here."

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:48.250
A lot of people don't train
this area on your leg.

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:50.160
You know, it's that soft meat, your calf,

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.000
or even right below your ankle.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.270
It's like hitting with a bat.

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:56.300
So I absolutely think leg
kicks can win a fight.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:59.135
A great movie, but it's not even close.

00:07:59.135 --> 00:08:00.430
So I'ma give it a 5.

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:02.717
Sorry, bro. Still love you.

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:14.090
As you see, Keanu is just evading,

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:16.090
and that's one of the key things too.

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:18.335
If someone's either sporadic or wild

00:08:18.335 --> 00:08:21.470
or they're just throwing,
it's good to kind of evade.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:22.940
I was taught a long time ago,

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:25.130
you can never fight with rage.

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:26.140
It just blinds you.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:28.240
I've done it once in my
career, and it cost me,

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:29.937
and I can say that plays a part

00:08:29.937 --> 00:08:31.603
in her just attack, attack, attack.

00:08:31.603 --> 00:08:32.520
There's the opening.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.610
And he blocked and defended it.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.950
He grabbed ahold of her
and gave her a judo throw.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:40.150
It's genius, where someone
keeps coming at you,

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:42.365
you just use their momentum to throw them.

00:08:46.244 --> 00:08:47.460
Yeah, he's holding her back,

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.160
but she could've easily
kicked him, throw some knees.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.252
She has her legs. She
wasn't really using that.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:57.850
There is no pause in a
real fight like that.

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.850
I wouldn't be like, "You OK?"

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:01.450
You know, you grab it, you break.

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:04.375
But other than that, you know,
this was just phenomenal.

00:09:04.375 --> 00:09:07.160
Credit to Keanu for
doing all of his stunts.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:08.627
I would give it a 10 out of 10.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:16.240
There's definitely some
funny moments. [laughs]

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:18.340
And as a dynamic striker,

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.495
one of the key things I always look for

00:09:21.495 --> 00:09:23.611
is how to perfect your technique,

00:09:23.611 --> 00:09:26.310
and any type of swinging motions like this

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.560
is usually an act of desperation

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:30.370
or usually I'm out of my element.

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:33.570
But we'll give it to him
because dramatic effect.

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:35.340
Dirty boxing is more than that.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:36.403
It's not just punching.

00:09:36.403 --> 00:09:38.947
My head is in your face. I'm pushing you.

00:09:38.947 --> 00:09:40.110
It's uncomfortable.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:43.550
It's just so uncomfortable
that you just want it to stop.

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:45.583
You can't breathe. You can't fight

00:09:45.583 --> 00:09:47.716
the way you want to fight.

00:09:47.716 --> 00:09:49.932
Very, very genius move,

00:09:49.932 --> 00:09:52.630
but if you don't know how
to get out of dirty boxing,

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:54.690
then you fall victim.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:57.090
Commentator: Definitely
not a crowd favorite.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:02.320
When homeboy fell, no one just
falls and holds their legs.

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:06.900
You fall in MMA longer for
more than two or three seconds,

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:09.230
that is considered almost like a knockout.

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:10.871
If I have a hurt leg, I'm not going to

00:10:10.871 --> 00:10:11.821
show you I have a hurt leg.

00:10:11.821 --> 00:10:13.460
I'm probably going to kick
you with that hurt leg.

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:15.530
Because the moment my
opponent recognize that,

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:16.810
he's going to exploit it.

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:18.830
Granted, I think what this movie is,

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:20.490
is some underground fighting.

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:22.340
So there's no rule to apply.

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:25.985
But in the real UFC, a ref will see that.

00:10:25.985 --> 00:10:28.803
Commentator: Big shot!
Epstein's in trouble.

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:33.000
I love the jujitsu part on the floor.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.293
I love how they were just back and forth,

00:10:35.293 --> 00:10:37.713
because that's jujitsu.
You're back, you're forward,

00:10:37.713 --> 00:10:39.320
one move doesn't work, you go to the next.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:40.620
You create chain moves.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:43.053
From how they were doing transitions

00:10:43.053 --> 00:10:44.818
to one move to the next

00:10:44.818 --> 00:10:46.883
shows that they have an
idea of what's going on.

00:10:50.106 --> 00:10:54.540
I think he's turning into a
omoplata, another common move.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:56.273
It's so hard to get out of.

00:10:56.273 --> 00:10:58.150
It's one of those move where you're stuck,

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:00.260
and if you don't really
know how to get out of it,

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:01.500
you can get hurt.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:04.110
So, for me, I'm not the
best grappler in the world.

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:06.240
So if I get there, I know how to get out

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.180
and how to maneuver myself to
get back to my strong suits.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.710
What I would naturally
try to do is roll over.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:13.185
If you notice, the person is going to

00:11:13.185 --> 00:11:15.728
stop you automatically from rolling over.

00:11:15.728 --> 00:11:17.145
And this is just one of those position

00:11:17.145 --> 00:11:18.369
where it's a stalemate or it's like,

00:11:18.369 --> 00:11:20.610
all right, who's going to impose the will

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:23.510
to either keep the person down or get out?

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:24.879
Commentator: That shoulder!

00:11:24.879 --> 00:11:26.412
[shoulder cracking]

00:11:26.412 --> 00:11:28.570
I noticed he finished him at the end.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:30.260
I'm a strong person.

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:32.545
If someone gets me in a omoplata,

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:35.398
I don't see someone finishing me

00:11:35.398 --> 00:11:38.448
by pressing down so hard
it'll pull my shoulder out.

00:11:38.448 --> 00:11:40.150
I'm speaking for myself.

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:42.120
I would give this a 9 out of 10.

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.985
The jujitsu part here was
really well done, I think.

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:53.300
Well, I'm a big fan of Michael Jai White.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:56.880
I actually learned a kick
from Michael Jai White.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:58.690
He has, what, seven black belts?

00:11:58.690 --> 00:11:59.940
So that guy's actually legit.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:03.850
So, fighting is like dancing,
and it's about rhythm.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:06.870
And right now these guys are
feeling each other's rhythm.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:09.070
Each opponent has the
counter for that move.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:11.010
He throws a technique,
he blocks, he hit back,

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:12.640
he counters, he goes back.

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:15.220
And it's just such a
beautiful piece to see.

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.920
Like, this I love. To
me, this is fighting.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:19.380
This is getting in the zone.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:20.840
This won't last for long, of course,

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:23.180
because eventually someone
will break their rhythm,

00:12:23.180 --> 00:12:24.505
whether it's fear,

00:12:24.505 --> 00:12:26.496
whether it's none of my stuff is working,

00:12:26.496 --> 00:12:28.000
whether it's this person's better than me,

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:29.963
or whether them getting clipped.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:37.547
So, what we're seeing right now

00:12:37.547 --> 00:12:40.860
is him getting kind of a
cross choke from behind.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:43.970
If his body was more attached
to Michael Jai White,

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:45.580
it would've been a little stronger,

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:47.440
but his upper body's more here.

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:49.810
So he's not latched on properly.

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:51.328
So that helps Michael Jai White

00:12:51.328 --> 00:12:53.288
get out of that move a little easier.

00:12:56.319 --> 00:12:57.650
I love this whole,

00:12:57.650 --> 00:13:00.540
my hand is up, I kick you
low, and I come back high.

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:01.610
I do it all the time.

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:04.340
I don't do a back fist.
I'm more of do a jab,

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:06.420
but this is Michael Jai
White we're talking about.

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:10.495
Faking is one of my best traits to use

00:13:10.495 --> 00:13:13.230
as I'm competing, because it interrupts.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:16.750
I can create that opening.
And if I create that opening,

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:19.050
then I can exploit my opponent's weakness.

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:21.216
And once I get you to react,

00:13:21.216 --> 00:13:23.457
then I can counter you properly.

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:31.060
Now, this is what fighting
should look like almost. Almost.

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:35.327
I think that it's similar to what I do

00:13:35.327 --> 00:13:38.330
with the prettiness of
the art of fighting,

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:40.171
the dynamic way of striking,

00:13:40.171 --> 00:13:42.870
but it is too crisp and pretty.

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:46.210
There's not going to be too
many standing in one spot.

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:48.280
There's going to be a lot
more movement involved

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:49.730
in a real situation.

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:52.250
You know, there's going to
be creating more openings,

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:55.730
more grabbing the leg, a
little bit of dirty-boxing-ish

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:59.900
to kind of create that
mixture of mixed martial arts.

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:02.383
But it just looks too clean.

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:09.220
The only way I can see
something like this working

00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:11.310
is because of the size difference.

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:12.943
So, putting that leg up,

00:14:12.943 --> 00:14:16.468
that opponent is feeling
that weight distribution,

00:14:16.468 --> 00:14:18.251
and he just rolls.

00:14:18.251 --> 00:14:19.790
But it depends how you roll, though.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:20.990
And if you roll backwards,

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:22.310
then you're rolling out of the move.

00:14:22.310 --> 00:14:24.318
So it's great for TV,

00:14:24.318 --> 00:14:27.326
but I wouldn't think a move like this

00:14:27.326 --> 00:14:29.340
would really work in real life.

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:32.710
I would give this a 9 out of 10.

00:14:32.710 --> 00:14:35.630
It's right there, but it's just not that,

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:37.923
it's not going to be
that pretty, pretty much.

00:14:40.505 --> 00:14:43.083
[laughing] Oh, my God.

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:45.260
This is so awkward.

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:48.460
Now I gotta judge myself?
Way to go, guys. Good job.

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:56.950
Yeah. This was a cool scene, man.

00:14:56.950 --> 00:14:58.401
I got to work with one of the best,

00:14:58.401 --> 00:15:00.668
but I think from what
I've learned from acting

00:15:00.668 --> 00:15:01.876
is that a lot of times

00:15:01.876 --> 00:15:03.768
when you throw those punches that way,

00:15:03.768 --> 00:15:05.890
it's not just straight to your face.

00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:07.123
Depending on the camera angle,

00:15:07.123 --> 00:15:09.830
it's going to look very good for TV.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:12.054
But for the untrained
eye, it's like, "Wow!"

00:15:12.054 --> 00:15:14.720
But for the rest of the
OGs, it's like, "What?"

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:22.810
Stance is everything.

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:25.790
My stance is another line of my defense.

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:27.081
You know, I might open enough

00:15:27.081 --> 00:15:29.956
where I can protect the
four vulnerable areas,

00:15:29.956 --> 00:15:33.297
such as my head, my neck,

00:15:33.297 --> 00:15:35.497
my solar plexus, and my groin.

00:15:35.497 --> 00:15:36.720
And it makes a straight line.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:39.720
That 45-degree angle
most of the time you see

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:40.990
is protecting that.

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:42.973
Right now we're kind of
feeling each other's rhythm.

00:15:45.693 --> 00:15:47.540
Yeah. I love this part right here.

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:49.281
Well, not when I die, but.

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:53.217
Oh!

00:15:54.191 --> 00:15:56.724
Went out like a G!

00:15:56.724 --> 00:16:00.553
I mean, you would have to be very accurate

00:16:00.553 --> 00:16:02.495
to kick someone like that,

00:16:02.495 --> 00:16:06.650
because your kicks are naturally
heavier than your arms.

00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:09.250
Plus my chin is a little
closer. It's protecting that.

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:11.040
But for this scene, you know,

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:12.306
he didn't kick me in the neck.

00:16:12.306 --> 00:16:17.300
We actually took his shoe
and someone put their fist

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:20.970
and put it at my neck to
make it look like a kick.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:22.660
Movies, man. Movies.

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:25.830
All right. I gotta keep
it real with myself.

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:28.350
Again, it goes back to
that too clean, right?

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:29.570
If it's going to be more scuffle,

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:31.602
like the part where he grabbed me

00:16:31.602 --> 00:16:33.170
and I was throwing some knees,

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:35.490
it's going to be a little
more realistic like that.

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:37.440
I'm going to give myself a 9 out of 10.

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:46.272
Using the environment in
close quarters like that,

00:16:46.272 --> 00:16:49.890
just tossing each other,
bouncing off of it,

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:51.540
I feel like this is a real fight.

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:56.370
When someone grabs your throat,

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:57.860
what a lot of people don't realize is that

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:00.910
you're stronger up here, but
as you go down, you're weaker.

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:02.930
So your wrist is naturally weak.

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:06.410
So anything that involves
me putting pressure on this

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:08.070
will alleviate that pressure.

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:10.970
Instinctively grabbing
something to hit that person,

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:13.403
I feel that works very well.

00:17:19.840 --> 00:17:22.100
Using the place, using the curtains,

00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:23.920
using anything they can find.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:25.370
So I rate it 10 out of 10,

00:17:25.370 --> 00:17:28.553
because it's the closest
thing to a realistic fight

00:17:28.553 --> 00:17:30.786
on the street or in a house.

00:17:30.786 --> 00:17:32.002
Commentator: Jackie's got the timing down,

00:17:32.002 --> 00:17:34.273
now can she control right here?

00:17:35.573 --> 00:17:37.348
This, red flags.

00:17:37.348 --> 00:17:39.006
You don't want to turn away, of course.

00:17:39.006 --> 00:17:41.110
You turn away, you're giving up your back,

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:42.500
and you get rear naked choked.

00:17:42.500 --> 00:17:45.030
Just like we saw from the other cartoon,

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.110
you want your legs
wrapped around the person.

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:49.680
So what I'm supposed to
do is push my hips back,

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:51.130
bring my knee in,

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:53.730
and then try to wrap my
leg around the person.

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:56.220
But she has one knee up,
which means it's hard

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:58.990
for the person's to cross over.

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:00.380
So that's a natural defense.

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:01.387
Commentator: Side control position,

00:18:01.387 --> 00:18:02.934
looking to get to the mount.

00:18:03.851 --> 00:18:05.246
Full mount.

00:18:05.246 --> 00:18:06.380
So, if you're in that position,

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:07.970
you never want to step over.

00:18:07.970 --> 00:18:10.550
Remember what I said about
grappling being like this?

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:13.240
If she steps over, that creates space.

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:15.128
And if anyone sees that on the bottom,

00:18:15.128 --> 00:18:17.760
they're going to push
and get their leg wrap.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:21.420
You want to slide your knee
across their stomach, like this.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:23.770
So my knee's going to be crossing over

00:18:23.770 --> 00:18:25.593
to get the mount position.

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:33.538
It's a lot easier for the person on top

00:18:33.538 --> 00:18:35.330
to cover the person on the bottom's mouth.

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:38.300
The person on top have more
ways to move their head

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:39.610
than the person on the bottom.

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:43.320
But what I think she's trying
to do is posture her up.

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:45.961
If I can push you away a little bit,

00:18:45.961 --> 00:18:47.435
then I can create space.

00:18:47.435 --> 00:18:50.620
But this is risky, because
the moment I do that,

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:52.870
I set myself up for an arm bar.

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:56.828
Commentator: She hops
on her back, standing.

00:18:56.828 --> 00:18:59.553
This rear naked is in! She has it.

00:18:59.553 --> 00:19:01.320
You get caught here, it's a finish.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:02.380
You go night-night.

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:05.420
This is if not the hardest
thing to get out of.

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:07.857
What happened is when you
put a rear naked choke in,

00:19:07.857 --> 00:19:08.790
think of it like a snake.

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:10.673
You kind of snake around the neck.

00:19:10.673 --> 00:19:13.270
If I can get the crease
of this part of my arm

00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:15.610
right in the middle
here, then I'm locked in.

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:17.930
What I like to do when I put this through,

00:19:17.930 --> 00:19:20.830
I grab my own shoulder like
this. Now I'm locked in.

00:19:20.830 --> 00:19:22.640
I think Halle did a great job.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:24.448
She snuck her arm in,

00:19:24.448 --> 00:19:26.706
and she got the other arm in like this.

00:19:26.706 --> 00:19:28.682
And all she had to do was squeeze,

00:19:28.682 --> 00:19:32.910
and basically cutting off the
blood circulation to the head.

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:35.029
You know, I think Halle Berry did

00:19:35.029 --> 00:19:39.779
such a great job in performing this MMA.

00:19:39.779 --> 00:19:42.640
There was some small spatial stuff,

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:45.370
but I don't think it's
too much of a big deal.

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:47.761
I'm going to probably give it, like,

00:19:47.761 --> 00:19:49.094
8 out of 10.

00:19:49.094 --> 00:19:52.110
I think my favorite scene
would be "Black Widow."

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:54.180
If you take away the octagon

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:56.400
and you take away all the rules and stuff,

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:58.760
a real scuffle would be like that,

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:01.300
where they're using
the entire environment.

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:02.980
That is the closest thing that I've seen

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:05.146
to anything being real

00:20:05.146 --> 00:20:07.179
from a mixed-martial-arts perspective.

00:20:07.179 --> 00:20:09.520
So "Black Widow."

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:11.550
Thanks for watching. If
you enjoyed this video,

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:13.741
I'm sure you'll like that one too.

